49	// Every person on the sheet in the order they were added, with their name shown as "<name> (<grade in force today>)", grade (the one in force on the sheet's today), claimed (the
50	// sum of their OPEN and SETTLED claims, leaving out corrections), due-total (the
51	// sum of their counting claims' already-rounded dues, at most the sheet's cap — their excess shows how far the sum is above it, else 0.00; n/a when their grade has no rate today and no settled or stated claim keeps a figure),
52	// their due-total for each period (fixed when the period was closed, 0.00 for a person added after),
53	// their score (the dues of their counting SETTLED claims that correct nothing, over 100, minus 5 per overdue counting claim, rounded half to even to a whole number) and its band
54	// (HIGH, MID or LOW by the sheet's score thresholds; blank while either is),
55	// their settled-total and open-total (the dues of their counting SETTLED claims, and of their counting OPEN and PENDING
56	// claims; together the sum of their figures before the cap),
57	// the dues of their counting OPEN and PENDING claims by age on today (0 to 30 days, 31 to 60, 61 or more; dated after today is 0 to 30),
58	// whether they are retired and from when, whether they have any hold, and their holds (a hold
59	// takes none of their claims out of any figure or total; it only gives them no cut in the pools), their overdue count and
60	// aliases (sorted as names are compared, comma and space separated), whether they clash (their name or an alias is used by someone else on a sheet of the tree), patrons (each as <name> <share>, sorted by name, comma and space separated; shares split 100.00 equally in the order named, rounded half to even, the remainder to the first-named) and backing (each patron's settled figure — their SETTLED claims dated within the 90 days up to and including today, at their frozen figures — times their share over 100, added up and rounded half to even; 0.00 with none),
61	// their overdue count and overdue-total (how many of their counting claims are overdue, and the sum of those claims' dues with their late charges, n/a adding nothing), their statements (the statement button, how many were taken, the last one's due-total and date) and their claims; then the
62	// sheet's claimed-total, due-total (the people's due-totals added up, skipping n/a), settled-total, open-total and cut total (everyone's added up), overdue count and overdue-total (the
63	// due-total, overdue-total, settled-total, open-total and cut total leave out the single highest and lowest person's figure when three or more have one).
64	// Then, for every tag ever used on the sheet's claims, its due-total: the dues of the counting claims carrying it. Retired
